               "summary":  "Qualcomm jumped 10.3% to $165.38 on November 23, 2026, riding a semiconductor-wide relief rally triggered by Nvidia reinstating CEO Jensen Huang rather than company-specific news. Earlier, Q4 FY26 earnings on November 4 beat estimates at $10.44B revenue and $2.17 non-GAAP EPS, yet shares slid as handset chip sales fell 26% YoY amid memory shortages and Apple\u0027s modem exit. Still, record $1.99B automotive sales (+89% YoY), a SpaceX 5G infrastructure deal, and a $5B FY27 data-center AI target show its pivot gaining traction while licensing stays locked in.",
               "technical":  "Despite jumping 10.3% to $165.38 on November 23, 2026, Qualcomm\u0027s technical structure remains bearish. The stock sits below all three exponential moving averages (EMAs, which track trend direction): EMA 50 ($172.42), EMA 200 ($172.69), and EMA 100 ($174.24). With EMA 50 marginally below EMA 200, the $172–$174 cluster forms firm overhead resistance. Momentum is stabilizing but not yet positive. The MACD (12/26/9, a trend-momentum gauge) sits at -3.99, below its -2.98 signal line, leaving a negative -1.02 histogram. Meanwhile, RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum oscillator where under 30 is oversold) recovered to 47.25, just under the neutral 50 mark. On volatility, price bounced off the lower Bollinger Band (20/2, a volatility envelope) at $151.03—near initial support—toward the $169.14 midline, with upper-band resistance at $187.24. At 36% below its $259.92 52-week high (low $121.99), QCOM sits in its lower third. Strong volume of 20.24M shares (32% above the 15.32M 20-day average) confirmed buying interest, though reclaiming $169–$174 is needed to shift the trend."
